            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Averages the values of a tensor: adds them up and divides by how many there are - the whole tensor, or each row or each column.\n\nThe mean is the everyday \"typical value\", the balance point of the data: one number for a frame of sensor readings, the centre of a cloud of points, or each joint averaged over a window of frames (dim 0 of a frames by joints tensor). Every value counts equally, so one wild value pulls it a long way - t.median is the outlier-proof choice.\n\nTHE dim ARGUMENT:\nWith no argument the WHOLE tensor is reduced to one value. A number typed after the name (t.mean 0) chooses a dim to reduce along and adds a dim inlet and a keep_dims checkbox to the node - it is the argument that creates them, so a node made without one cannot be given a dim later (retype it). For a table of rows and columns, dim 0 reduces DOWN each column (one answer per column) and dim 1 ALONG each row (one answer per row). The dim named is the one that disappears: a 6 by 8 tensor gives 8 answers on dim 0 and 6 on dim 1. Negative numbers count from the end: -1 is the last dim.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.mean <dim: int>\nDefault: no dim - the whole tensor.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.mean 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor. Every one that arrives is averaged and the answer sent - this is the only inlet that makes the node send. A list or a NumPy array is first turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu; a tensor is used as it is, on its own device, and the answer stays there. An empty tensor is ignored: nothing is sent.\nA tensor of WHOLE NUMBERS (int64, or the bool mask a comparison gives) is refused: torch prints \"mean(): could not infer output dtype\" and nothing is sent. Put a t.to set to float32 in front. (A list or NumPy array of whole numbers is fine - it arrives as float32.) A NaN anywhere makes the answer NaN; t.nanmean skips them.\n\ndim (inlet, only when an argument was given):\nA whole number. Changing it does NOT send by itself; the next tensor uses it. A dim the tensor does not have (2 or more for a table, or 1 for a 1-D tensor) is an error: the console prints \"Dimension out of range\" and nothing is sent.\n\nkeep_dims (checkbox, only when an argument was given; default off):\nOff, the reduced dim is removed: 6 by 8 reduced on dim 0 has shape [8]. On, it is kept with length 1: shape [1, 8] on dim 0, [6, 1] on dim 1. Keep it when the answer must line up against the original again - to subtract each row's mean from that row, for instance.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nWith no dim, one number (a 0-dimensional tensor). With a dim, a tensor with that dim removed (or kept at length 1 with keep_dims). Same dtype and device as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.rand makes 6 rows by 8 columns of values between 0 and 1, written in the cells; click its button for new ones. The plain t.mean beside it averages all 48 - always near 0.5. The slider sets the dim of the second node: it goes through 't bang int', so the number reaches the dim inlet first and the bang then makes the var re-send the held tensor - a dim change alone would not send. t.info shows the shape of the answer: [8] for dim 0, [6] for dim 1. At dim 0\neach cell of the strip is the mean of one column; at dim 1, of one row (row 0 on the left).\n\nRELATED:\nt.median is the middle value, which ignores outliers. t.nanmean skips NaN values. t.sum is the total before dividing. np.mean is the NumPy version, with an axis menu instead of a dim argument.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
